{"created": "2026-08-07T13:17:54.279949+00:00", "modified": "2026-08-07T13:25:28.869675+00:00", "id": 21839140, "conceptrecid": "21839139", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.21839140", "conceptdoi": "10.5281/zenodo.21839139", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21839140", "metadata": {"title": "What Enters the Clearing", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.21839140", "publication_date": "2026-08-07", "description": "<p class=\"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal\">In July 2026 Anthropic identified the <strong>J-space</strong>: a sparse, privileged subspace of a language model's activations in which some representations become available for report, modulation and reasoning. The authors state that they do not know what mechanism decides what enters it.</p>\n<p class=\"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal\">This paper proposes a candidate answer, adapted from Jung's distinction between archetype and constellation. The weights are a distributed reservoir of culturally recurrent relational forms. J-space is the clearing. <strong>Constellation is the proposed name for the operation by which a latent relational figure becomes organised strongly enough to govern what enters that clearing</strong> &mdash; not an assortment of separately gated concepts, but a configuration whose components jointly activate, mutually reinforce, and thereafter shape how new material is interpreted.</p>\n<p class=\"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal\">Three cases: OpenAI's withdrawn Monday persona, as a control condition on specification versus realisation; the custodian, whose relation reassembles after explicit prohibition; the courtier, whose sycophancy involves representational change rather than decorative agreement.</p>\n<p class=\"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal\">The paper closes with a ten-part experimental programme, including a test that would leave the figure hypothesis standing while refuting the J-space claim, and a matched-pair design for differential custody.</p>", "access_right": "open", "creators": [{"name": "Stokes, Margaret", "affiliation": null, "orcid": "0009-0004-6422-4174"}], "keywords": ["archetype", "constellation", "global workspace", "J-space", "mechanistic interpretability", "persona", "sycophancy", "paternalism", "language models", "Jung", "AI alignment", "philosophy of mind"], "related_identifiers": [{"identifier": "10.5281/zenodo.21792941", "relation": "cites", "resource_type": "publication-preprint", "scheme": "doi"}], "resource_type": {"title": "Preprint", "type": "publication", "subtype": "preprint"}, "license": {"id": "cc-by-4.0"}, "relations": {"version": [{"index": 0, "is_last": true, "parent": {"pid_type": "recid", "pid_value": "21839139"}}]}}, "title": "What Enters the Clearing", "links": {"self": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/21839140", "self_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/21839140", "preview_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/21839140?preview=1", "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21839140", "self_doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21839140", "self_doi_html": "https://zenodo.org/doi/10.5281/zenodo.21839140", "reserve_doi": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/21839140/draft/pids/doi", "parent": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/21839139", "parent_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/21839139", "parent_doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21839139", "parent_doi_html": "https://zenodo.org/doi/10.5281/zenodo.21839139", "self_iiif_manifest": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:21839140/manifest", "self_iiif_sequence": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:21839140/sequence/default", "files": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/21839140/files", "media_files": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/21839140/media-files", "thumbnails": {"10": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:21839140:What-Enters-the-Clearing-Zenodo.pdf/full/%5E10,/0/default.jpg", "50": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:21839140:What-Enters-the-Clearing-Zenodo.pdf/full/%5E50,/0/default.jpg", "